## Cross-cutting question

Nine of the fifteen items reduce to the same root cause: the plain-CSS layer lacks a React primitive's counterpart. `weft-components.css` has 19 classes against 96 React primitives, and a sandboxed panel iframe cannot import React — so templates re-implement.

**Comment on this line if the right follow-up is widening the plain-CSS layer** (`panel-header`, `hud-list-row`, `badge`, `stat-row`, `action-button-row`, `toggle-group`) rather than resolving these one at a time.

## Two open token questions

**T1 — `--weft-muted` and `--weft-info`.** The only genuine divergences between the reviewed mock's dark palette and canonical Weft 0.2.1: mock `#aab4bf` vs canonical `#8b93b0`, and mock `#c4b5fd` (violet) vs canonical `#93c5fd` (blue). The template takes canonical for both.

**Examples added 2026-07-29 on request.** The same board rendered with each candidate — only these two tokens differ. Dark theme only, since the divergence exists solely in the dark block.

![T1 token divergence — canonical vs mock, dark theme](data:image/jpeg;base64,)

**Measured contrast on the board surface** (`--weft-paper` dark `#141a2e`), AA small-text bar is 4.5:1:

| Token | Source | Hex | On paper | On cream | AA small text |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `--weft-muted` | canonical | `#8b93b0` | 5.67:1 | 6.22:1 | pass |
| `--weft-muted` | mock | `#aab4bf` | 8.21:1 | 9.01:1 | pass |
| `--weft-info` | canonical | `#93c5fd` | 9.57:1 | 10.50:1 | pass |
| `--weft-info` | mock | `#c4b5fd` | 9.35:1 | 10.26:1 | pass |

**All four pass, so this is not an accessibility call.** Two real questions:

1. **muted** — the mock is noticeably lighter, which promotes secondary meta; canonical holds a firmer hierarchy between title and meta. Aesthetic preference, and it affects every muted string on the board (sub-lines, rail labels, chips, captions).
2. **info** — canonical `#93c5fd` is blue and sits beside `--weft-link` `#5b87fd`, so a blue info chip can read as a link. The mock's violet is unambiguously not a link. **This is the strongest single argument in either direction**, and it argues for the mock on info specifically.

A defensible split: take **canonical muted** (keeps hierarchy) and **mock info** (avoids the link collision). **Comment on this line with the call.**

**T2 — rail control density.** The rail's controls are 34px against `--weft-control-h` (44px, or 36px under compact density). Held as a documented literal.

**DIRECTION 2026-07-29 (Katie).** *"Yes we want users to be able to opt for a high density interface."* This becomes a real density axis in Weft rather than a board literal. Two ways to land it — **open sub-question:**

- **(a) Add a third tier, `data-density="dense"` at 34px** *(recommended)* — existing `compact` consumers untouched, users get a genuine high-density opt-in, board stops carrying a literal.
- **(b) Tighten `compact` itself to 34px** — fewer moving parts, but shifts every surface already on compact, including Heddle's pixel-parity baselines.

Either path edits the density blocks in `css/weft.css` — the pure token file Heddle injects verbatim — so it needs the byte-stability and injection checks plus a token-layer version bump.

## Test cases for the decided work

Coverage spec for the decisions above. Written against this repo's actual conventions, not generic ones, so each case is directly executable. **Comment on any case to add, cut, or change it.** Cases marked **GUARD** encode a decision so it cannot silently regress — those are worth arguing about, because they are the difference between a decision and a comment in a doc.

Coverage spec for the fifteen decisions in *Weft board template — design-system decisions*
(D1–D15) plus T1 and T2. Written against this repo's actual conventions, not generic ones,
so each case is directly executable.

**Comment on any case to add, cut, or change it.** Cases marked **GUARD** encode a decision
so it cannot silently regress — those are the ones worth arguing about, because they are the
difference between a decision and a comment in a doc.

### The four layers

| Layer | Where | Run |
|---|---|---|
| Component unit | `src/ui/__tests__/<id>.test.tsx` | `npm test` |
| Visual regression | `tests/visual/gallery.spec.ts` + gallery cards | `npm run test:visual` |
| Contract gates | manifest, props snapshot, tokens, exports | `npm run verify`, `npm run props`, `node scripts/check-raw-colors.mjs`, `node scripts/check-pure-token-file.mjs`, `node scripts/check-exports-contract.mjs` |
| Downstream | Heddle consumes the bump | Heddle's `npm run linux:check` + its visual suite |

**Conventions observed in the existing 43 suites:** first line is `// @vitest-environment jsdom`;
`@testing-library/react` + `vitest`; assertions target `[data-slot="<id>"]` attributes and
className fragments. **There is no axe/jest-axe in this repo** — accessibility is asserted
through roles, accessible names and ARIA attributes in the same unit test. New cases follow
that, rather than introducing a new a11y dependency.

**Visual suite mechanics:** `gallery.spec.ts` iterates `light | dark | compact` over a
curated `SECTIONS` list, screenshotting `<section id="${id}-example">` from the gallery. A
changed primitive already in `SECTIONS` will move its baseline — that update must be
deliberate, never a reflex `--update-snapshots`.

---

### Group 1 — changes to existing primitives

These carry the highest silent-breakage risk: the primitive keeps its name and its consumers,
and its appearance moves under them. Every case here pairs a *new behaviour* test with a
*regression* test that pins what must not move.

#### D1 · `HudListRow` adopts the template's hierarchy

`src/ui/__tests__/hud-list-row.test.tsx`

- Title, meta and a third project line each render into distinct slots with distinct weight/size — assert three separate `data-slot`s exist rather than asserting pixel values.
- **REGRESSION:** existing state accents still apply — `default | unread | overdue | resolved | active` each still set their marker attribute/class.
- **REGRESSION:** `density="compact"` still tightens; `as="button" | "li" | "div"` still switch the element; `leading` / `trailing` slots still render.
- **REGRESSION:** `bodyClassName`, `align`, `divider`, `interactive`, `disabled` unchanged — the existing suite already covers several; extend rather than replace.
- **GUARD:** the third (project) line is optional — a row with only title + meta renders no empty third slot. Prevents a stray empty node in every existing consumer.
- Visual: `hud-list-row` is already in `SECTIONS`, so its light/dark/compact baselines change. Update deliberately and eyeball all three.
- Downstream: SignalRow, ticket updates and account lists consume this. Heddle's visual suite must be run before the version is pinned.

#### D2 · `StatRow` gains the board variant

`src/ui/__tests__/stat-row.test.tsx` (new file — no suite exists today)

- Default render is unchanged: `label` left, `value` right, optional `hint`.
- New variant renders the denser rhythm **and** exposes a leading slot.
- Leading slot accepts the D13 dot and renders it before the label.
- **GUARD / REGRESSION:** omitting the variant produces byte-identical output to today. This is the promise made to existing consumers; assert it explicitly.
- Props: additive surface → **minor** component bump + `npm run props:write`.

#### D3 · `ActionButtonRow` moves to the template's arrangement

`src/ui/__tests__/action-button-row.test.tsx` (extend)

- New default arrangement: primary + secondary grouped at the start, link-styled action pushed to the end.
- `align="start" | "end" | "between"` all still honoured.
- **GUARD:** the row does not style its children — passing `Button`s through leaves their variants intact. Stops the row growing button opinions, which is exactly the D3/D4 boundary.
- **REGRESSION:** existing consumers (Copy/Email/Vault rows) keep their current arrangement, or their change is explicitly accepted and snapshotted.

#### D5 · Count / space / status chips become `Badge` variants

`src/ui/__tests__/badge.test.tsx` (extend)

- Three new variants render: count pill, space tint, status outline.
- Existing 5 tones × 4 variants all still render — matrix test.
- **GUARD / REGRESSION:** `Badge` with no new prop is unchanged.
- Visual: `badge` is in `SECTIONS`; gallery card must show the new variants or they are untested visually.

#### D7 · `ToggleGroup` gains a `joined` variant

`src/ui/__tests__/toggle-group.test.tsx` (extend) and a CSS-layer case

- Joined variant: single shared border, inset ring on the active item, no gaps.
- Selected item carries `aria-pressed="true"` (or `aria-checked` per the underlying primitive) — the template's markup had this and it must not be lost.
- Keyboard: arrow-key traversal and Enter/Space activation still work in the joined variant.
- **REGRESSION:** `PillToggleGroup` gap-separated behaviour untouched.
- CSS layer: a `weft-components.css` counterpart exists so panel iframes get the joined look — assert the class is present in the shipped file (see the CSS-layer case in Group 4).

#### D8 · `Callout` gains dashed + band variants

`src/ui/__tests__/callout.test.tsx` (extend)

- Dashed variant renders a dashed border; band variant renders a filled full-width footer band.
- Both compose with the existing 5 tones and both densities — spot-check the combinations rather than the full matrix.
- `title`, `icon`, `action`, `markdown` slots still work in the new variants.
- **GUARD / REGRESSION:** default `Callout` unchanged.

#### D10 · `PanelHeader` gains a board-scale size

`src/ui/__tests__/panel-header.test.tsx` (new file)

- Default HUD-dense strip unchanged.
- Board size variant renders the larger title.
- `PanelHeaderTitle` / `PanelHeaderActions` / `PanelHeaderDismiss` all still compose; dismiss keeps an accessible name.
- **GUARD:** the board template no longer defines its own header — `weft-board-head` is gone from `css/weft-templates.css` (see the deletion guard in Group 2).

---

### Group 2 — adoptions and deletions

The risk here is the opposite: the old thing lingers. Each case includes a **deletion guard**
asserting the template-local class is actually gone, so we do not end up shipping both.

#### D4 · Delete `weft-board-btn`; use `Button size="sm"` + dense variant

- `src/ui/__tests__/button.test.tsx` (extend): the dense variant renders 12.5px type and the 2px radius; existing 6 variants × 4 sizes matrix still renders; `loading`, `blocked`, `disabled`, `pressed` states unaffected.
- **DELETION GUARD:** `css/weft-templates.css` contains no `weft-board-btn` selector.
- **GUARD (a11y):** a link-styled control is still a `<button>` — assert `role="button"`, not `link`, for the drawer action. This is the caveat raised in the D4 review and it is the one most likely to be lost in implementation.

#### D6 · Adopt `SourcePill` for the mono chip

- `src/ui/__tests__/source-pill.test.tsx` (new file): renders mono type; `truncate` ellipsises long ids; `tone="muted"` applies.
- **DELETION GUARD:** no `weft-board-type` selector in `css/weft-templates.css`.
- **GUARD:** D5's chips and D6's mono chip stay distinct — a test that both a `Badge` space chip and a `SourcePill` render in the same row without colliding class names. This encodes the "chip split" note so implementation cannot conflate them.

#### D11 · Real `Input` / `Textarea` in the rail and drawer (defect fix)

This is the only decision fixing a live defect, so it gets the most direct assertions.

- The rail search is a real `<input>`: appears via `getByRole('textbox')`, accepts typed value, exposes an accessible name.
- The drawer reply is a real `<textarea>` with the same three assertions.
- **GUARD (focus):** both show a visible focus ring on `:focus-visible`. The old divs had none — assert the focus affordance exists rather than trusting the global rule.
- **DELETION GUARD:** no `weft-board-rail-search` or `weft-board-drawer-reply` div-styling selectors remain in `css/weft-templates.css`.
- Keyboard: Tab reaches both in DOM order.

---

### Group 3 — new primitives promoted into Weft

Each of these needs the **full new-component bar**: unit suite, manifest entry (ordered) with
semver `version`, props snapshot, gallery card, and light/dark/compact review.

#### D12 · Urgency-toned tier group

`src/ui/__tests__/<tier-group-id>.test.tsx`

- Three tones render distinct container treatments: blocked, awaiting, fyi.
- Header composes label + sub-label + count.
- Count slot accepts a `Badge` (D5) and the dot slot accepts the D13 dot.
- Semantics: the group is a landmark/region with an accessible name from its label, and the count is announced as part of it — not an orphaned number.
- **GUARD (doctrine, D9-adjacent):** a tier with zero items does not render an empty shell. Encodes "never render an empty tier set", the doctrine most likely to be lost.
- **GUARD:** tone is semantic, not positional — a tier does not infer its tone from DOM order.
- Visual: new gallery card; add to `SECTIONS` so all three tones are pinned in three themes.

#### D13 · Standalone status dot

`src/ui/__tests__/<dot-id>.test.tsx`

- Four tones render: ok, warn, stop, info.
- **GUARD (a11y):** the dot is decorative by default — `aria-hidden="true"`, no accessible name, because the adjacent label carries the meaning. If a caller passes a label it becomes an `img`/`status` role with that name. Colour alone must never be the only signal.
- Renders inline without disturbing baseline alignment of adjacent text.
- Unblocks D2 — one test composing the dot into the `StatRow` leading slot.

#### D14 · Copyable reference row

`src/ui/__tests__/<ref-row-id>.test.tsx` — the only promotion with behaviour, so the heaviest suite.

- Long id truncates with ellipsis while the full value stays available (title/`aria-label`).
- Copy control has an accessible name (not a bare icon).
- Clicking copies: assert `navigator.clipboard.writeText` called with the full id, mocked via `vi.spyOn`.
- **Success affordance:** after a copy the control announces success (text swap and/or `aria-live`), then reverts. Assert both the appearance and the revert.
- **Failure path:** clipboard rejects → no false success shown. Easy to forget; clipboard fails in insecure contexts.
- Keyboard: control is reachable and activates on Enter/Space.

#### D15 · Evidence chip on `Badge` + provenance doctrine

- `direct | alias | trace` each render a distinct chip built on `Badge`.
- **GUARD (never-invent provenance):** a row with no evidence renders **no chip at all** — not a default, not an "unknown" chip. This is the single most important test in the document: it is the only place the never-invent rule becomes machine-checked rather than a review habit.
- **GUARD (a11y):** the three kinds are distinguishable without colour. T1 kept canonical `--weft-info`, which sits close in hue to `--weft-link`, so `direct` and `trace` are both blue — assert a non-colour differentiator (text is sufficient today; if an icon is added, assert it).

---

### Group 4 — tokens and the plain-CSS layer

#### T1 · Canonical tokens only — **GUARD**

T1 decided to keep canonical Weft values, which makes this cheap and permanent:

- **GUARD:** `css/weft-templates.css` contains **zero custom-property declarations**. It may only *read* `var(--weft-*)`. Verified true today (0 declarations), so this locks in both T1 and the "a template never pins a palette" rule. A node test over the file, or a line in `check-pure-token-file.mjs`'s sibling checks.
- Every `var(--weft-*)` the template reads resolves in `css/weft.css` / `css/theme.css` — catches typo'd token names, which nothing currently catches.
- `node scripts/check-raw-colors.mjs` stays clean over `css/weft-templates.css`.

#### T2 · Density axis — **branch pending**

The recommended path (a) adds a third `data-density="dense"` tier at 34px:

- `--weft-control-h` resolves 44px default, 36px compact, 34px dense.
- **REGRESSION:** every surface currently on `compact` is unchanged — the whole point of choosing (a) over (b).
- Visual: extend `gallery.spec.ts`'s theme loop with `dense`, giving a third baseline per section.
- `check-pure-token-file.mjs` still passes — the new tier must be a plain `:root[...]` axis block with no component selectors.
- Byte-stability: `css/weft.css` is injected verbatim by Heddle, so the diff must be additive only.

*If (b) is chosen instead, replace the regression case with an explicit accept-list of every compact consumer whose baseline moves, including Heddle's.*

#### Cross-cutting · plain-CSS counterparts

Nine of fifteen decisions traced to the CSS layer being thin. Each class added there needs a
presence + parity case:

- `.weft-btn.is-link` (D4), plus counterparts for `panel-header`, `hud-list-row`, `badge`, `stat-row`, `action-button-row`, `toggle-group` if the widen-the-layer follow-up is taken.
- For each: the class exists in the shipped `css/weft-components.css`, is token-only, and renders equivalently to its React counterpart in light and dark.
- **GUARD:** `check-exports-contract.mjs` still resolves every subpath from the packed tarball — currently 16.

---

### Gate matrix

| Decision | Unit | Visual baseline moves | Manifest bump | Props snapshot | Token gates |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| D1 HudListRow | extend | yes (in SECTIONS) | yes | if surface changes | — |
| D2 StatRow variant | new file | yes if carded | minor | yes (additive) | — |
| D3 ActionButtonRow | extend | yes if carded | yes | if surface changes | — |
| D4 Button dense + delete | extend | yes (in SECTIONS) | yes | yes (additive) | raw-color |
| D5 Badge variants | extend | yes (in SECTIONS) | minor | yes (additive) | — |
| D6 SourcePill | new file | — | — | — | raw-color |
| D7 ToggleGroup joined | extend | yes if carded | minor | yes (additive) | raw-color (CSS layer) |
| D8 Callout variants | extend | yes (in SECTIONS) | minor | yes (additive) | — |
| D9 notice standard | pending branch | pending | pending | pending | — |
| D10 PanelHeader size | new file | yes if carded | minor | yes (additive) | — |
| D11 Input/Textarea fix | new cases | yes (in SECTIONS) | — | — | raw-color |
| D12 tier group | new file | new baseline | **new entry** | new entry | raw-color |
| D13 status dot | new file | new baseline | **new entry** | new entry | raw-color |
| D14 reference row | new file | new baseline | **new entry** | new entry | raw-color |
| D15 evidence chip | extend badge | yes if carded | minor | yes (additive) | — |
| T1 canonical tokens | guard test | — | — | — | **all** |
| T2 dense tier | — | **+1 theme, all sections** | token-layer bump | — | pure-token-file, byte-stability |

**Rule that constrains all of it:** `props:write` refuses to record a breaking surface change
unless the component's manifest `version` **major** was bumped. Removing a prop or a variant,
or making a prop required, is breaking. `--force` is only for extractor changes — never for a
contract change.

---

### The three regressions most likely to bite

Not exhaustive — the ones where the test above is doing real work rather than ceremony.

1. **D1 changes a primitive that four Heddle surfaces already render.** The unit tests will
   pass while SignalRow silently reflows. The protection is the downstream run, not the unit
   suite. Do not pin the Weft version in Heddle until Heddle's visual suite is green.
2. **D2/D5/D8's "default unchanged" promise.** Easy to assert, easy to forget, and it is the
   entire basis for calling these additive minor bumps rather than majors.
3. **D11's focus ring.** The old markup had no focus affordance at all. A test that only
   checks `getByRole('textbox')` passes without proving the defect is fixed.

### Open items that change these cases

- **T2 branch** — new `dense` tier (recommended) vs tightening `compact`. Changes whether the visual suite gains a fourth theme or re-baselines every compact consumer.
- **D9 branch** — whether the introduced notice replaces `EmptyState` wholesale or only for load-failure/notice states. Wholesale means test cases across Knowledge Search, Notes and every other consumer.

## Decision log

- 2026-07-29 · **D1 decided (reversed my recommendation).** `HudListRow` adopts the template's hierarchy — the template's version reads with better hierarchy. Keeps HudListRow's state accents and density variants. Changes a shipped primitive's appearance: needs a component version bump; props snapshot moves too if it lands as a variant rather than the default.
- 2026-07-29 · **D2 decided.** Board treatment becomes an additive `StatRow` variant (leading dot slot + denser rhythm); current default preserved so existing consumers are untouched. Minor component bump + props-snapshot refresh.
- 2026-07-29 · **D3 decided (reversed my recommendation).** `ActionButtonRow` moves to the template's arrangement rather than the template adopting the current row.
- 2026-07-29 · **D4 decided (as recommended).** Delete `weft-board-btn`; buttons become `Button size="sm"` with the dense type/radius as a `Button` variant. Reconciles with D3 — template wins on arrangement, Weft wins on the control.
- 2026-07-29 · **D5 decided (reversed my recommendation).** Count / space / status chips keep the introduced treatment — judged more readable. Landing as `Badge` variants rather than board-local so future panels inherit them.
- 2026-07-29 · **D6 decided (as recommended).** Mono type chip adopts `SourcePill`, gaining truncation and a muted tone.
- 2026-07-29 · **Chip split, stated explicitly:** D5 keeps the introduced count / space / status chips; D6 moves only the mono type chip to `SourcePill`. Both are chips — spelled out so implementation does not conflate them.
- 2026-07-29 · **D7 decided.** Joined segmented look becomes a variant of the existing Weft toggle (`ToggleGroup` + plain-CSS counterpart); `PillToggleGroup` stays the gap-separated default.
- 2026-07-29 · **Emerging principle (D2, D5, D7).** The template's treatments *graduate into* existing primitives as variants — they do not fork them, and the primitive's current appearance stays the default. Carried into D8–D15 unless overridden.
- 2026-07-29 · **D8 decided (reversed).** Dashed hint box and filled provenance band kept, landing as `Callout` variants.
- 2026-07-29 · **D9 decided (reversed) — with an open sub-question.** The introduced notice becomes the standard. `EmptyState` has consumers beyond the board (Knowledge Search, Notes), so pending confirmation the split is: introduced treatment for load-failure/notice, centered shape retained for true empties.
- 2026-07-29 · **D10 decided (as recommended).** Adopt `PanelHeader`; board title becomes a size variant if wanted.
- 2026-07-29 · **D11 decided (as recommended).** Display-only divs replaced with real `Input` / `Textarea` — a defect fix, restoring focus, keyboard entry and accessible roles.
- 2026-07-29 · **D12, D13, D14 decided (all as recommended): promote to Weft.** Urgency-toned tier group, standalone status dot, copyable reference row. D13 unblocks the D2 `StatRow` variant; D14 is the only promotion carrying behaviour (truncation + clipboard), so it needs a copy-success affordance and an accessible control name.
- 2026-07-29 · **D15 decided (as recommended).** Evidence chip built on `Badge`; provenance vocabulary and the never-invent rule stay doctrine, not component API.
- 2026-07-29 · **T1 DECIDED: keep canonical Weft tokens** for both `--weft-muted` (`#8b93b0`) and `--weft-info` (`#93c5fd`). No token change; the mock's `#aab4bf` / `#c4b5fd` are dropped, retiring the last piece of the mock override block. Consequence accepted: `.is-direct` (link blue) and `.is-trace` (info blue) sit close in hue — if that bites, the fix is a non-colour differentiator on the chips, not reopening the token.
- 2026-07-29 · **D4 link variant shown on request.** Coverage confirmed: `variant="link"` handles the drawer's trailing action and the board's three other link-styled controls, no template-local button needed. Two notes carried forward — keep link-styled controls as `<button>` for a11y, and add `.weft-btn.is-link` to close the CSS-layer gap.
- 2026-07-29 · **T1 examples produced on request** — side-by-side render plus measured contrast. All four values pass AA (lowest 5.67:1), so accessibility does not decide it. Open; a defensible split is canonical muted + mock info, since canonical info sits beside `--weft-link` and can read as a link.
- 2026-07-29 · **T2 direction given:** users should be able to opt into high density, so this becomes a real density axis. Open sub-question: new `dense` tier (recommended) vs tightening `compact`.
- 2026-07-29 · **Answered on D4:** `Button variant="link"` exists in React (`src/ui/button.tsx:22`); the plain-CSS layer has no `.weft-btn.is-link`, which is why the template invented one. Added to the widen-the-CSS-layer list. The template's `margin-left:auto` on the link is layout, so it belongs to D3's `ActionButtonRow` pattern, not the button.
- 2026-07-29 · **No blanket adopt/reject rule.** D1, D3 replace the primitive's treatment; D2, D5, D7 add a variant; D4, D6 adopt the existing primitive outright. Per-item. **All fifteen decisions (D1–D15) are now recorded.** **All decisions are now closed.** T1: keep canonical tokens. **T2 DECIDED 2026-07-30 (Katie): path (a) — both tiers exist.** `compact` keeps 36px untouched and a new `dense` tier adds 34px, so a surface opts into high density rather than compact changing under existing consumers. **D9 DECIDED 2026-07-30 (Katie): the narrow split ships here; the holistic change is spun off** to weft#12 and will not be delivered in this PR.
